// CRES Academy question bank, units 9 and 10. All questions are original.
// Fields: id, t (lesson id), q (stem), a (four options), c (index of correct option), e (explanation).
window.FRA = window.FRA || {};
FRA.questions = FRA.questions || [];
FRA.questions.push(
// u9l1
{id:"u9l1-1",t:"u9l1",q:"A technologist reports that room 2 is \"not working\" and walks away. What is your FIRST step?",a:["Order the most commonly failed board for that generator","Get the symptom in the operator's words with the room, the exam, and the step where it happens, then reproduce it","Open the generator cabinet and measure the internal supplies","Take the room out of service until the vendor can attend"],c:1,e:"Not working is not a symptom. The method starts by recovering what was actually seen, with the exam, the step, and how often, and then reproducing it yourself, because a fault you cannot reproduce is still only a report."},
{id:"u9l1-2",t:"u9l1",q:"Before removing any cover on a system that threw an intermittent fault last week, which resource should you read?",a:["The department's technique chart","The manufacturer's marketing specification sheet","The service history in the CMMS and the system's own error log","The purchase order and warranty terms"],c:2,e:"History is step two of the method. The service record and the error log tell you what changed and what preceded the fault, and what changed is the most useful question in troubleshooting."},
{id:"u9l1-3",t:"u9l1",q:"You can produce a dark abdominal image at the console yourself only when the outer ionization chamber is selected on a small patient. What does that finding indicate?",a:["A generator kVp calibration fault","A detector gain calibration fault","A high-voltage cable termination fault","A use error, whose corrective action is a demonstration and applications training"],c:3,e:"A device error reproduces at a fixed technique with the operator removed. When the fault only appears with a particular operator selection, the machine is behaving as designed and the fix is a demonstration and training, documented like any other corrective action."},
{id:"u9l1-4",t:"u9l1",q:"Isolating a fault by halves in an imaging room means splitting the system into which set of blocks?",a:["Power, control, high voltage, mechanical, receptor, and network","Console, cabinet, table, and tube stand","Hardware, software, firmware, and network","Inpatient, outpatient, emergency, and mobile"],c:0,e:"Each split lets you prove which side is good and halves what is left to search. The blocks follow the signal: power in, control through the console, high voltage to the tube, mechanics and geometry, the receptor, then the network path out."},
{id:"u9l1-5",t:"u9l1",q:"Two careful attempts have not moved a fault that lives inside a sealed high-voltage assembly you cannot open. What should you do?",a:["Keep working the fault until it is solved, because escalation reflects badly on the shop","Escalate to the manufacturer with your readings, the error text, and what you tried","Return the room to service and watch for the fault to recur","Swap parts from an identical room until the symptom moves"],c:1,e:"Escalation is a decision, not a defeat. It is correct when the assembly is sealed, the tool or licence is not yours, the part is not field-replaceable, or two honest attempts have not changed the symptom, and it belongs in the record with everything you proved."},

// u9l2
{id:"u9l2-1",t:"u9l2",q:"Three calls arrive at once: a cosmetic panel on a scanner, a radiographic room whose measured kVp is 18 percent off while it is still imaging patients, and a chest stand with a drifting detent. Which comes FIRST?",a:["The cosmetic panel, because it is quick","The drifting detent, because it affects every exam","The kVp fault, because the room is outside tolerance and still in use","All three on the same visit, in arrival order"],c:2,e:"Measured kVp must sit within 10 percent of the indicated value. A system outside its limits and still imaging patients comes out of service first; convenience and workflow items are logged and scheduled."},
{id:"u9l2-2",t:"u9l2",q:"A collimator lamp has burnt out in a busy radiographic room. How should that finding be classified?",a:["A cosmetic item, because the x-ray field is unchanged","A receptor fault, because the field no longer matches the detector","A workflow item that can wait for the next preventive visit","A dose issue, because the technologist will guess the field"],c:3,e:"A dark lamp does not change the beam, but it invites an uncollimated or mis-centered exposure, so the patient receives the difference. Light field and x-ray field agreement within 2 percent of the SID is a dose requirement, and a dark lamp defeats it."},
{id:"u9l2-3",t:"u9l2",q:"The hospital's only computed tomography scanner and the third of three radiographic rooms both go down on the same morning. Which should you work first, and why?",a:["The scanner, because no alternative exists for the work it carries","The radiographic room, because it is the simpler repair","Whichever vendor can ship a part fastest","The radiographic room, because it serves more patients per day"],c:0,e:"Downtime is weighed by whether an alternative exists, not by the size of the queue. A sole scanner reroutes patients out of the building, while a third radiographic room has two working neighbours."},
{id:"u9l2-4",t:"u9l2",q:"What does taking an imaging system out of service properly require?",a:["A verbal warning to the technologist on duty","A tag the staff cannot miss, the energy secured where the hazard is electrical or mechanical, a work order with the reason and the measurement, and notice to the manager","An entry in the department's daily log at the end of the shift","Removal of the exposure hand switch only"],c:1,e:"Out of service is a documented act. The tag, the secured energy, the recorded measurement that drove the decision, and the notification are what a surveyor reads later, and the system returns on verification readings in the record rather than on a feeling."},
{id:"u9l2-5",t:"u9l2",q:"A rented mobile radiographic unit arrives to cover a two-week repair. What must happen before it images a patient?",a:["Nothing, because the rental company certifies it before shipping","A single test exposure on a phantom","Incoming inspection, electrical safety testing, output verification, and network configuration","Only a visual inspection and a wipe-down"],c:2,e:"A loaner is a new device to your facility. Incoming inspection, electrical safety, output verification, and the network setup are part of the downtime estimate you give radiology, not an afterthought."},

// u9l3
{id:"u9l3-1",t:"u9l3",q:"You need the sequence of events that happens inside a generator during an exposure. Which part of the service manual carries it?",a:["The parts list and exploded views","The specification tables","The installation requirements","The theory of operation"],c:3,e:"Theory of operation is where the block diagram and the exposure sequence live, which is what you need to decide where to look. Adjustments, error codes, parts, and tolerances each sit in their own section."},
{id:"u9l3-2",t:"u9l3",q:"A room shows no exposure and no error code at all. Which document gets you to the cause fastest?",a:["The schematic, so you can follow the interlock chain contact by contact","The error code table","The parts list for the exposure switch assembly","The acceptance test report from installation"],c:0,e:"No code with no exposure points at the series interlock chain, where any open contact blocks the exposure. The schematic shows the chain and the test points; the code table has nothing to offer when there is no code."},
{id:"u9l3-3",t:"u9l3",q:"Service software on a modality shows a long error log and you want to clear it to see new faults. What should you do first?",a:["Clear it, because old entries only confuse the picture","Export or record the log before clearing it","Clear it and ask the vendor for a copy from their server","Leave it alone, because logs may never be touched"],c:1,e:"The entries before a fault are the evidence that explains it, so clearing the log destroys the sequence. Export first, then compare timestamps with the room's schedule, which is how a first-exposure-of-the-morning or protocol-specific fault reveals itself."},
{id:"u9l3-4",t:"u9l3",q:"A calibration procedure in the manual lists prerequisites you have not completed. Why does the order matter?",a:["It does not; calibrations are independent of each other","The manufacturer voids the warranty if steps are reordered","Later adjustments depend on earlier ones, so out of order means doing them twice","The service software will refuse any out-of-order step"],c:2,e:"Calibrations build on each other, which is why a kVp calibration done before a sagging line voltage is corrected has to be repeated. Read the whole procedure and the specification table before touching the first adjustment."},
{id:"u9l3-5",t:"u9l3",q:"You are about to call the manufacturer's technical support about an unfamiliar fault code. What should you have in hand?",a:["The department's annual budget for that system","The original purchase date and installation photographs","The names of the technologists who reported the fault","The model, serial number, software version, exact error text, your readings, and what you already tried"],c:3,e:"Support can only match a fault to a published fix with the version and the exact error text, and your readings stop them walking you back through checks you have done. Ask for open field notices on that model while you have them on the line."},

// u9l4
{id:"u9l4-1",t:"u9l4",q:"A radiographic room makes no exposure and the console displays no error code at all. What is the MOST likely cause?",a:["An open contact somewhere in the interlock chain","A failed high-voltage transformer","A defective flat-panel detector","A tube that has exceeded its anode heat limit"],c:0,e:"No exposure with no code points at the series interlock string: door, emergency stop, rotor ready, receptor parked and size selected, collimator or tray switch, and the exposure switch itself. Thermal and high-voltage faults announce themselves with a code."},
{id:"u9l4-2",t:"u9l4",q:"Every mA station in a room measures low on output, and the fault appeared after electrical work in the building. Which check comes FIRST?",a:["The kVp calibration in service mode","Incoming line voltage measured under exposure load","The detector gain calibration","The AEC chamber selection"],c:1,e:"Every calibrated value moves with the supply, so a sagging or loose feed makes all stations read low. Prove line voltage under load before adjusting a calibration, or the calibration will be wrong once the feed is fixed."},
{id:"u9l4-3",t:"u9l4",q:"The rotor hums during preparation but the system never reaches the ready state. Where should you look?",a:["The anode cooling curve and the heat unit load","The automatic exposure control chamber","The stator drive board, the run capacitor, and bearing drag","The collimator blade position sensors"],c:2,e:"Ready depends on the rotor reaching speed. A hum without reaching ready is a stator drive or run capacitor fault, or mechanical drag, while a noisy or slow run-up with a healthy drive is bearing wear, which means the tube itself."},
{id:"u9l4-4",t:"u9l4",q:"Repeated identical exposures on a room vary in output by about 12 percent, and the console reports no fault. How should that be treated?",a:["Acceptable, since no code was thrown","A receptor problem rather than a generator problem","Acceptable, because the limit is 20 percent for repeated exposures","Out of tolerance, because reproducibility allows no more than plus or minus 5 percent"],c:3,e:"Reproducibility allows no more than plus or minus 5 percent over repeated identical exposures, and linearity allows no more than 10 percent variation in output per mAs between adjacent stations. A system outside those limits comes out of service regardless of whether it threw a code."},
{id:"u9l4-5",t:"u9l4",q:"Before working inside an imaging system's high-voltage tank, which sequence is correct?",a:["Open the disconnect, lock and tag it, wait the stated discharge time, then ground the terminals with the grounding stick","Switch the console off, then remove the tank cover","Discharge the terminals with a screwdriver blade, then open the disconnect","Leave the system energized so you can observe the arc"],c:0,e:"Stored energy in the high-voltage section is lethal after the console is off. Lockout, the manufacturer's stated discharge time, and the grounding stick on the high-voltage terminals come before contact, and nobody works alone in an energized tank."},

// u9l5
{id:"u9l5-1",t:"u9l5",q:"Images from one table Bucky show fine parallel lines across the whole field. What is the MOST likely cause?",a:["A stale detector gain calibration","A grid that is not moving during the exposure","Patient motion during a long exposure","An incomplete erase of the imaging plate"],c:1,e:"Grid lines appear when a reciprocating grid stands still, so the strips are recorded instead of being blurred out. Check the Bucky drive, its motor, and the switch that starts it before suspecting the receptor."},
{id:"u9l5-2",t:"u9l5",q:"A new grid is fitted and the images now show normal density in the center with pale edges on both sides. What happened?",a:["The grid ratio is too low for the kVp used","The tube is off center to the grid","The grid is upside down","The detector needs a new offset calibration"],c:2,e:"An inverted focused grid cuts the beam increasingly toward the edges, so the center survives and the margins fade. Off level drops density everywhere and off center drops it at the sides, which is how the three errors are told apart."},
{id:"u9l5-3",t:"u9l5",q:"A computed radiography image shows a faint second anatomy underneath the current exam. What is the cause?",a:["Scatter from an adjacent room","A grid frequency beating against the sampling","A defective row of detector elements","Incomplete erasure of the plate from a previous image"],c:3,e:"A ghost is a previous image still on the plate, so the plate is re-erased and repeated ghosts point at the reader's erase lamps. A plate left idle for 24 hours also collects background radiation and is erased again before use."},
{id:"u9l5-4",t:"u9l5",q:"Every image from one room is slightly unsharp, the geometry checks out, and bone edges are soft everywhere rather than in one direction. Which cause fits BEST?",a:["Focal spot growth or an exposure time that is too long","Patient motion during the exposure","A dead column in the detector","A grid that is off level"],c:0,e:"Motion blurs in a direction and receptor faults are local or patterned, but a grown focal spot blurs every edge uniformly. Measure the exposure time, then expose a phantom at a fixed technique to separate the tube from the generator."},
{id:"u9l5-5",t:"u9l5",q:"You want one test that tells a receptor fault from a generator fault from a tube fault. Which do you run?",a:["Replace the detector and see whether the complaint stops","Expose a phantom at a fixed technique on the suspect receptor, then on a known good receptor in the same room","Compare images from two different rooms on the same patient","Run the service software self-test and accept its verdict"],c:1,e:"If the fault follows the receptor it is the receptor; if it stays with the room, measured kVp and mAs decide: wrong numbers are the generator, correct numbers with poor sharpness are the tube. One test, three answers, and a number for the report."},

// u9l6
{id:"u9l6-1",t:"u9l6",q:"A technologist reports that the overhead tube column drifts slowly downward when the vertical lock is set. What should you do FIRST?",a:["Adjust the detents and return the room to use","Lubricate the vertical rails and retest","Take the system out of service and secure the load","Log it for the next preventive maintenance visit"],c:2,e:"A suspended component that creeps is a counterweight cable, pulley, or brake fault, and it can drop. Locks, brakes, counterweights, and collision sensors are safety items, so the system comes out of service immediately with the load secured."},
{id:"u9l6-2",t:"u9l6",q:"A beam-alignment check shows the light field sitting 4 cm off the radiation field at a source-to-image distance of 100 cm. Is that acceptable?",a:["Yes, because the limit is 5 cm at that distance","No, but the room may stay in use until the next scheduled visit","Yes, provided the technologist collimates generously","No, because the limit is 2 percent of the SID, which is 2 cm at 100 cm"],c:3,e:"Light field to x-ray field agreement is held within 2 percent of the SID, which is 2 cm at a 100 cm SID, and automatic collimation plus the SID indicator are held to the same 2 percent. Outside that, the field cannot be trusted and the room comes out of use."},
{id:"u9l6-3",t:"u9l6",q:"A powered table tilt motion runs partway and then stops in the same place every time. What is the MOST likely cause?",a:["A limit switch, position sensor, or loose connector","A failed drive motor","Low hydraulic fluid in the column","Worn tabletop ways"],c:0,e:"A motion that stalls repeatedly at the same point is usually being told to stop rather than failing to pull, so check the limit switches, position sensors, and their wiring before condemning a motor or a pump."},
{id:"u9l6-4",t:"u9l6",q:"A digital room throws a no-exposure condition only when the wall stand is selected, and nothing is wrong electrically. What should you check?",a:["The high-voltage cable terminations","Whether the wireless panel is seated in its holder so the system knows it is parked","The anode cooling curve","The grid ratio fitted to the wall stand"],c:1,e:"Receptor parked and size selected sit in the interlock chain, so a panel out of its holder or a tray not latched blocks the exposure. That is one of the most common no-exposure calls in a digital room and costs nothing to check."},
{id:"u9l6-5",t:"u9l6",q:"While lubricating a tube stand you find the manual does not mention the brake surface. What is the correct action?",a:["Apply a light film of the same grease to prevent squeal","Use a dry lubricant instead, since it will not migrate","Lubricate only what the manual names, with what it names","Lubricate it and record the deviation in the work order"],c:2,e:"Grease on a brake, clutch, or drive wheel is a failure you created, and it is a safety failure. Lubricate only what the manual names and record play, noise, and effort at every visit so wear is trended instead of discovered."},

// u9l7
{id:"u9l7-1",t:"u9l7",q:"A flat-field image from a digital detector shows smooth shading and faint banding across the panel. What fixes it?",a:["Replacing the detector","Updating the modality's software","Cleaning the detector cover","Running the gain and offset calibration"],c:3,e:"Calibration measures each pixel's response to a uniform beam and corrects smooth, repeatable errors such as shading, banding, and background mottle. Physical damage and growing dead clusters are not reachable by calibration and mean replacement."},
{id:"u9l7-2",t:"u9l7",q:"A fluoroscopic image has become dim and noisy, and the measured tabletop dose rate at a fixed phantom has climbed over the last year. What is the MOST likely cause?",a:["An aging image intensifier losing conversion gain","A failed collimator lamp","A grid fitted upside down","A detector gain calibration that has drifted"],c:0,e:"As an intensifier loses gain, automatic brightness control raises technique to defend brightness, so dose rate rises before the picture looks bad. That is why a tabletop dose rate baseline is the most valuable number in a fluoroscopy record."},
{id:"u9l7-3",t:"u9l7",q:"A fluoroscopic image rotates slightly and shows distortion at its edges. Which receptor is in use?",a:["A flat-panel detector, which distorts at its margins","An image intensifier, which rotates and distorts as it ages","A photostimulable storage phosphor plate","A film-screen cassette"],c:1,e:"Rotation and edge distortion are intensifier signatures, because the image is carried by electrons through a vacuum and focused electrostatically. Flat panels do not rotate or distort at the edges; they fail in rows, columns, and panels, and they lag when hot."},
{id:"u9l7-4",t:"u9l7",q:"Measuring a fluoroscope, you read 24 R/min at the tabletop in boost mode. What does that reading mean?",a:["It is within limits, since boost mode is unrestricted","It is acceptable if the exam is short","It exceeds the 20 R/min boost limit, so the system comes out of service","It only matters if the five-minute timer has failed as well"],c:2,e:"Tabletop exposure rate is held to 10 R/min in normal operation and 20 R/min with the boost feature, so 24 R/min is out of service until corrected. Check the brightness control calibration, a magnification mode left engaged, continuous rather than pulsed operation, and a missing filter."},
{id:"u9l7-5",t:"u9l7",q:"You have replaced a detector interface board in a digital radiographic room. What closes the job?",a:["A single clinical image reviewed by the technologist","A note in the CMMS that the board was replaced","A visual inspection of the cabling","A flat-field image, a phantom image at a fixed technique against the baseline, the dose indicator in range, and the numbers recorded"],c:3,e:"A receptor repair is verified with the tests that would have caught the fault, compared against the baseline on file. Without a baseline a phantom image proves nothing, which is the practical reason the quality control record exists."},

// u9l8
{id:"u9l8-1",t:"u9l8",q:"A mammography unit reaches only 18 lb of compression force measured with a scale in both powered and manual modes. What does that mean?",a:["It is below the required 25 to 40 lb range, so the system comes out of service until corrected","It is acceptable because the technologist can add manual force","It is acceptable because the limit applies only to powered mode","It indicates a detector calibration fault"],c:0,e:"Compression must reach and hold 25 to 40 lb in both powered and manual modes, and the system must not be able to exceed 40 lb automatically. A unit that cannot reach the force is a drive, a force sensor, or a calibration fault."},
{id:"u9l8-2",t:"u9l8",q:"The weekly mammography phantom score has fallen by 0.8 from the established value. What is the correct response?",a:["Increase technique until the score returns to baseline","Stop and investigate calibration, target and filter selection, compression, and processing","Repeat the phantom image until a passing score is obtained","Record the drop and continue, since the limit is 1.0"],c:1,e:"A score drop of more than 0.5 is an investigation trigger, not an invitation to adjust technique. Adjusting technique to make the phantom look right hides the fault and raises dose to every patient imaged afterward."},
{id:"u9l8-3",t:"u9l8",q:"A compression paddle will not release after an exposure on a mammography system. How is that classified?",a:["A workflow irritation to be scheduled","A paddle wear item to be replaced at the next visit","An immediate out-of-service fault, because it can trap a patient","An image quality fault"],c:2,e:"A paddle that cannot release traps the patient, so the system comes out of use at once and the release path including any manual override is tested. Cracked, chipped, or bent paddles are replaced rather than dressed."},
{id:"u9l8-4",t:"u9l8",q:"An ultrasound image shows a dark vertical stripe in the same place regardless of the preset. What is the FIRST test?",a:["Replace the system's beamformer board","Reload the system software","Increase the gain and depth settings","Scan a phantom, then move a known good probe to the same port"],c:3,e:"A stripe is element dropout or a broken cable conductor. The swap settles it: a fault that follows the probe to another port is the probe, and a fault that stays with the port is the system."},
{id:"u9l8-5",t:"u9l8",q:"Two ultrasound probes have failed within a month, both shortly after cleaning. What is the MOST likely cause?",a:["The wrong disinfectant, soaking past the immersion line, or a wet connector","A power supply fault in the cart","Excessive use of acoustic gel","An out-of-date software version"],c:0,e:"Immersion and chemistry damage is the most common avoidable probe failure. The fix is procedural: a visible immersion line, only the manufacturer's approved chemistry, a dry connector, and retraining the staff who clean, with a leakage test added for transesophageal probes."},

// u9l9
{id:"u9l9-1",t:"u9l9",q:"A battery portable fails to expose on the ward but works plugged in, and its pack reads full open-circuit voltage. What is the FIRST check?",a:["Replace the battery pack on the strength of the resting voltage","Measure pack voltage under load during drive and exposure","Reload the generator calibration","Inspect the collimator lamp circuit"],c:1,e:"A tired pack holds a convincing resting voltage and collapses at the first load. Measure under drive and exposure, log run time and exposures per charge, and prove the charger output and interlock before condemning cells."},
{id:"u9l9-2",t:"u9l9",q:"A mobile radiographic unit creeps forward when parked on a sloped corridor. How should you handle it?",a:["Advise staff to park it only on level floors","Log it for the next preventive maintenance visit","Take it out of service immediately and check the brake pads and adjustment","Reduce the drive speed setting"],c:2,e:"Brakes and the dead-man drive switch are safety systems on a machine of several hundred pounds that travels where patients walk. A unit that creeps or will not stop comes out of service at once."},
{id:"u9l9-3",t:"u9l9",q:"A C-arm reports a fault only when the arm is rotated to one particular orbital position. What should you suspect?",a:["A software fault that needs a reload","A grid misalignment at the detector","An exhausted battery pack","A cable flex failure, found with a continuity wiggle test"],c:3,e:"Transport and articulation break cables one conductor at a time, so a fault tied to a position is a flex failure. Put continuity on the suspect conductor, flex the cable through its range, and replace the cable or connector rather than splicing it."},
{id:"u9l9-4",t:"u9l9",q:"Portable chest images from one unit are consistently less sharp than department images, though the unit tests correctly. What explains it?",a:["Motion from long exposure times, grid misalignment, and an estimated rather than set distance","The portable generator is out of calibration despite the tests","The detector needs replacement","The collimator blades are out of adjustment"],c:0,e:"Mobile image faults are usually not system faults. A battery unit uses longer times, a hand-held or wedged detector tilts the grid, and the distance is often judged by eye instead of measured, which also changes magnification."},
{id:"u9l9-5",t:"u9l9",q:"After a mobile unit returns from an isolation room, what should the inspection include?",a:["Only a visual check that the surfaces look clean","Confirming the approved cleaning chemistry was used and inspecting the hand switch, rails, keyboard, and connectors for liquid intrusion","A full electrical safety test every time","Replacement of the detector cover"],c:1,e:"The commonest harm from cleaning is a soaked control or a wet connector, and the second commonest is the wrong chemistry. A unit damaged by cleaning product is a training finding, so confirm which bottle the staff are using."},

// u9l10
{id:"u9l10-1",t:"u9l10",q:"You have replaced an arcing high-voltage cable and verified output. What should you tell the lead technologist?",a:["The technical detail of the cable termination failure and the parts used","That the vendor has been contacted and a report will follow","What works now, what does not, and when the room comes back","Nothing until the service report has been filed"],c:2,e:"Clinical staff need three things in their own language so the schedule can move once. Technical detail belongs in the service report, and radiologists get the measurements that say the image can be trusted."},
{id:"u9l10-2",t:"u9l10",q:"Reproducing a complaint shows the system behaving exactly as designed when a particular chamber is selected. How do you handle the conversation?",a:["Tell the technologist they selected the wrong chamber and close the call","Change the console settings so the selection is no longer possible","Report the technologist to the department manager","Demonstrate the behaviour on the machine, arrange applications training, and document it without blame"],c:3,e:"Name the machine as the subject of the sentence, show the setting that produces the result and the one that does not, and record the corrective action like any other. A repeat of the same call means the training is still an open item."},
{id:"u9l10-3",t:"u9l10",q:"You are escalating a fault to the manufacturer. Which set of information should be ready before the call?",a:["Model, serial number, software version, exact error text, your readings, and what you tried","The purchase price and the department's annual budget","The names of everyone who used the room this week","A photograph of the console display only"],c:0,e:"Published fixes are version specific and the exact error text is what matches a known defect, while your readings stop support walking you back through checks you have done. Ask for open field notices on that model while you are on the line."},
{id:"u9l10-4",t:"u9l10",q:"Which set of items belongs in a completed imaging service report?",a:["The symptom and the parts used only","The symptom as reported, the finding, the action, parts by number, verification readings with tolerances, and the return-to-service time","A narrative description of the repair with no numbers","The vendor's case number and nothing else"],c:1,e:"The report answers three later questions at once: did the repair work, is this device failing repeatedly, and was the system safe when it went back into use. Dose and quality control results belong in it when output, geometry, or the receptor was touched."},
{id:"u9l10-5",t:"u9l10",q:"A modality cannot send images and you believe the network switch port is at fault. How should you hand it to the information technology team?",a:["Ask them to check everything on that floor","Open a ticket saying the modality is broken","Give the device address, the tests you ran, the results, and the time the fault started","Wait until the vendor confirms the modality is healthy"],c:2,e:"Evidence rather than suspicion is what gets another department to act. The address, the test, the result, and the start time let them look in one place, and the exchange is recorded in both ticket systems."},

// u10l1
{id:"u10l1-1",t:"u10l1",q:"A new console works perfectly with the other devices in its own room but cannot reach anything elsewhere in the hospital. Which setting should you check FIRST?",a:["The port number used by the archive","The subnet mask only","The name server entries","The default gateway"],c:3,e:"The gateway is the exit to other networks, so a wrong gateway leaves a device able to talk to its own subnet and nothing beyond it. Users describe that as the whole network being down from one console."},
{id:"u10l1-2",t:"u10l1",q:"Why are modalities and archives given static addresses rather than addresses handed out automatically?",a:["Every other node is configured to find them at one address and port, so a changed address breaks all senders","Static addresses are faster than leased ones","Automatic addressing is not supported by imaging equipment","Static addresses avoid the need for a subnet mask"],c:0,e:"A lease is right for a workstation and wrong for a node other devices must find. If the archive's address changed overnight, every sending room would stop at once, so these addresses come from a documented plan kept with the equipment record."},
{id:"u10l1-3",t:"u10l1",q:"A workstation shows an address beginning 169.254 and cannot reach anything. What does that indicate?",a:["A failed imaging application","It asked for a leased address and nothing answered","A duplicate static address on the network","A wrong subnet mask entered by the installer"],c:1,e:"A self-assigned address in that range means the device requested a lease and received no reply, which points at the link, the port's VLAN, or the address service, never at the imaging application itself."},
{id:"u10l1-4",t:"u10l1",q:"A modality can reach the archive when the address is typed in but fails when the archive's name is used. Where is the fault?",a:["The switch port","The subnet mask","Name resolution","The archive's disk space"],c:2,e:"Works by address and fails by name is name resolution, without exception. That is why some older modality entries are still written as addresses and keep working when the name service is down."},
{id:"u10l1-5",t:"u10l1",q:"Imaging equipment is usually placed on its own logical network segment. Which set of reasons explains that?",a:["It is cheaper to cable and easier to label","It allows modalities to use leased addresses safely","It removes the need for a firewall between imaging and the rest of the hospital","Traffic volume, narrow access rules, and modalities running software that cannot be patched on the enterprise schedule"],c:3,e:"A VLAN separates traffic on shared switches. Imaging earns its own for the size of its transfers, the specific rules it needs, and because validated software stacks cannot be patched on anybody else's timetable. The practical trap is a good cable in a port on the wrong VLAN."},

// u10l2
{id:"u10l2-1",t:"u10l2",q:"A scanner needs a 10 Gbps link to the archive over a copper run of about 80 m. Which cable and connector fit?",a:["Cat 6A with an RJ45 connector","Cat 5e with an RJ45 connector","Coaxial cable with a screw connector","Single-mode fiber with an optical connector"],c:0,e:"Cat 5e supports 1 Gbps and Cat 6A supports 10 Gbps, both on RJ45 connectors with a 100 m channel limit that includes the patch cords at each end. Fiber is for distance beyond that and for runs near heavy electrical noise."},
{id:"u10l2-2",t:"u10l2",q:"There is no link light at either the modality's network card or the switch port. What does that tell you?",a:["The address is wrong on the modality","The fault is physical: the patch cord, the jack, the run, or the port","The archive has refused the association","The switch port sits on the wrong VLAN"],c:1,e:"No light at either end settles the physical layer in seconds. A light with no activity means connected and silent, which moves the fault up to addressing or the application."},
{id:"u10l2-3",t:"u10l2",q:"Sends from one radiographic room take minutes instead of seconds, with no error message anywhere. What should you suspect?",a:["A failed detector","An expired certificate on the archive","A link that negotiated at a lower speed than it should, usually a damaged pair","A wrong AE title at the modality"],c:2,e:"A degraded link produces no error, only slowness, and it is usually a damaged pair in the cord or the run. The department reports it as a slow archive, which sends everyone looking in the wrong place."},
{id:"u10l2-4",t:"u10l2",q:"Every wireless detector in the building lost its link on the same afternoon, and nothing changed in any imaging room. What is the MOST likely cause?",a:["All the detector batteries aged out together","Interference from a single microwave oven","Simultaneous failure of each portable unit's network card","A change made centrally at the wireless controller"],c:3,e:"Access points are configured centrally, so one controller change applies to all of them at once. That architecture produces a characteristic fault: every wireless client in the building affected with nothing changed locally."},
{id:"u10l2-5",t:"u10l2",q:"Which fault does a cable tester prove, and which question must still go to the network team?",a:["It proves continuity, wiring, shorts, split pairs, and length, but not which VLAN a port is in","It proves the VLAN assignment but not the wiring","It proves the archive is reachable but not the cable length","It proves the link speed but not continuity"],c:0,e:"A tester works on the cable itself and the better ones report the distance to a fault. VLAN assignment, port errors, and firewall rules are read from the switch and the firewall, so they are the two questions worth asking early."},

// u10l3
{id:"u10l3-1",t:"u10l3",q:"An archive's storage array reports that it is degraded after a drive failure, and the department is still working normally. How should you treat it?",a:["As a completed self-repair, since the array is still serving data","As a failure already in progress whose margin is gone, needing urgent replacement","As a backup copy that can now be relied on","As a performance problem to be scheduled with the next upgrade"],c:1,e:"A RAID set survives a drive and warns you, but a degraded array has lost its safety margin and a second failure takes it. It is also not a backup: an array deleted, encrypted, or destroyed takes every copy inside it."},
{id:"u10l3-2",t:"u10l3",q:"The hospital's patch management team wants to push this month's operating system updates to a computed tomography console. What is the correct answer?",a:["Approve it, since security patches always take priority","Approve it after taking a backup of the console","Patches go on only when the manufacturer has validated them for that software version","Decline all patching permanently on medical devices"],c:2,e:"A modality's operating system is validated as part of the product, so patches follow the vendor's validated schedule. The gap until a validated patch arrives is covered by segmentation and controlled access rather than by patching anyway."},
{id:"u10l3-3",t:"u10l3",q:"A workstation powers up and reports that no boot device was found. What are the two usual causes?",a:["A failed power supply or a dead fan","A missing network cable or a wrong address","Insufficient working memory or a hot processor","A failed drive or a changed boot order, often a drive left in a port"],c:3,e:"That message means the firmware found nothing bootable, so suspect the storage device itself or the boot order, including removable media left plugged in. A machine that powers up with no picture is a display, cable, or graphics fault instead."},
{id:"u10l3-4",t:"u10l3",q:"Reconstruction times on a scanner's acquisition workstation have roughly doubled since a software upgrade. Which explanation fits BEST?",a:["A resource limitation in processing or working memory on that workstation","A detector gain calibration that has drifted","A failing high-voltage cable","An expired certificate on the archive interface"],c:0,e:"Reconstruction is arithmetic on large arrays, so it is limited by the processor, the working memory, and the disk. Slow reconstruction after a software change is a resource complaint, and the detector only enters the picture when image quality changes."},
{id:"u10l3-5",t:"u10l3",q:"Several imaging servers run as virtual machines on one physical host. What risk does that arrangement add?",a:["Virtual servers cannot be backed up","One host failure takes down every server running on it","Patching is no longer required on virtual servers","Virtual servers cannot hold patient data"],c:1,e:"Consolidation, fast provisioning, and snapshots are the gains; the shared exposure is the host. Clustering and tested backups still matter, and virtual servers still need patching on the vendor's terms."},

// u10l4
{id:"u10l4-1",t:"u10l4",q:"The local disk on one radiographic room fills every week and studies have to be cleared by hand. Which archive function should you check?",a:["Prefetching of prior studies","The hanging protocols on the reading stations","Storage commitment from the archive","The routing rules to the subspecialty readers"],c:2,e:"Storage commitment is the archive confirming it has taken permanent responsibility for a study, which is what makes clearing the local copy safe. When commitment fails quietly, nothing can be cleared automatically and local disks fill."},
{id:"u10l4-2",t:"u10l4",q:"A radiologist reports that prior studies are missing when today's examination opens. What is the MOST likely cause?",a:["The archive has lost the prior studies","A hanging protocol assigned to the wrong display","A failed detector calibration on the original study","A prefetch or routing rule, or an identifier mismatch that stopped the priors being recognized"],c:3,e:"Prefetching and routing move priors toward the reading station before the patient arrives. Missing priors are nearly always a rule or an identifier matching problem, not lost data, which is why the first question is whether the priors exist at all."},
{id:"u10l4-3",t:"u10l4",q:"Yesterday's studies cannot be found by any reading station, while today's work is sending and displaying normally. Which part of the system fits that pattern?",a:["The database and its index","The archive storage itself","The reading workstations","The modality interfaces"],c:0,e:"Images survive a database failure but nobody can find them, which is exactly the pattern described. Naming which of the four parts (archive, database, workstations, interfaces) you are in before touching anything is the most useful habit in imaging informatics."},
{id:"u10l4-4",t:"u10l4",q:"A radiologist says images from one modality always come up in the wrong places on the displays. Whose configuration is that?",a:["The modality's detector calibration","The hanging protocol, configured by the application team","The archive's storage commitment settings","The switch port's VLAN assignment"],c:1,e:"A hanging protocol is the saved layout deciding which series appears where. A wrong layout is configuration, often combined with how the modality labels its series, and recognizing it saves you troubleshooting an archive that is working."},
{id:"u10l4-5",t:"u10l4",q:"The archive has been down for three hours and the department is still scanning patients. Which number should you be watching?",a:["The number of unread studies in the reading queue","The temperature in the archive room","Free local storage space on each modality","The number of open work orders"],c:2,e:"During an archive outage reading stops while acquisition continues into local storage, so the department is living on local disk. Knowing how many studies each room can still hold is what tells the manager when imaging has to stop."},

// u10l5
{id:"u10l5-1",t:"u10l5",q:"Two imaging nodes must negotiate an association before anything moves. Which four items have to agree?",a:["Address, subnet mask, gateway, and name server","Compression ratio, matrix size, bit depth, and transfer rate","Patient name, accession number, study date, and modality","AE title, host address, port, and supported SOP classes"],c:3,e:"Three of those four are typed by a human during installation, which is why a single mistyped character is the most common reason a new room cannot send. Ports are commonly 104 or 11112, and the titles are case sensitive."},
{id:"u10l5-2",t:"u10l5",q:"Which single service proves that two nodes can open an association at all, and is therefore the first test when images will not move?",a:["C-ECHO","C-FIND","C-STORE","C-MOVE"],c:0,e:"Verification proves the titles, the address, the port, and the path in one step. A failure means stop looking at the application; a success means the identities and the network are fine and the fault lies in what the nodes are trying to do."},
{id:"u10l5-3",t:"u10l5",q:"A new modality verifies successfully against the archive but every attempt to store an image fails. What should you suspect?",a:["A wrong AE title on the modality","A mismatch of supported SOP classes, or disk, licence, or permission limits at the receiver","A failed network cable between the room and the closet","A wrong subnet mask on the archive"],c:1,e:"Verification already proved the titles, address, port, and path. What remains is what the nodes can do and whether the receiver will accept the object, so compare the two conformance statements and read the archive's own log and alerts."},
{id:"u10l5-4",t:"u10l5",q:"What does a modality worklist query deliver to the console, and why does it matter for patient safety?",a:["The archive's free disk space, so studies are not lost","Prior studies for comparison, so the radiologist can read faster","The day's scheduled patients with their identifiers and accession numbers, so nobody types them","The hanging protocols, so images display correctly"],c:2,e:"Selecting from a worklist instead of typing is the department's main defence against misidentified studies. An empty worklist means hand-typed patients, and every one of those becomes reconciliation work in several systems afterwards."},
{id:"u10l5-5",t:"u10l5",q:"Before a new reading application is purchased, which document tells you whether it can actually exchange studies with your existing modalities?",a:["The preventive maintenance schedule","The manufacturer's security disclosure form","The purchase order and warranty terms","The conformance statement from each product"],c:3,e:"A conformance statement lists a product's application entity titles, supported service and object pairs, its roles, its transfer syntaxes, and its limits. Comparing two of them is how you discover before installation that two devices will not talk."},

// u10l6
{id:"u10l6-1",t:"u10l6",q:"Match the three message types to their jobs in a radiology department.",a:["ADT carries identity and location, ORM carries the order, ORU carries the result","ADT carries orders, ORM carries identity, ORU carries images","ADT carries results, ORM carries identity, ORU carries orders","All three carry images with different compression"],c:0,e:"Admit, discharge, and transfer messages tell every system who the patient is and where; the order message carries the requested exam and its accession number; the result message carries the dictated report back to the record."},
{id:"u10l6-2",t:"u10l6",q:"Which identifier ties one order to one study, and which ties studies to a person?",a:["The medical record number ties the order to the study; the accession number ties studies to the person","The accession number ties the order to the study; the medical record number ties studies to the person","Both jobs are done by the accession number","Both jobs are done by the study date and time"],c:1,e:"Those two identifiers hold the whole chain together, which is why duplicates, merges, name changes, early acquisition, and hand typing all show up as studies that will not attach rather than as network errors."},
{id:"u10l6-3",t:"u10l6",q:"Text has stopped flowing between the scheduling system and the archive, though both appear healthy. Where do you look first?",a:["The modality's detector calibration log","The switch port counters","The interface engine's queue depth and error log","The archive's hanging protocols"],c:2,e:"The broker receives, translates, routes, queues, and logs every message with a reason for each rejection. Its queue depth is the best single health indicator in the text chain, and its log names the failing field or the unknown patient."},
{id:"u10l6-4",t:"u10l6",q:"Put the order-to-report path in the correct sequence.",a:["Order, worklist, schedule, acquisition, report, archive","Worklist, order, schedule, archive, acquisition, report","Schedule, order, acquisition, worklist, archive, report","Order, schedule, worklist, acquisition, archive, read, report"],c:3,e:"A clinician orders in the record system, the scheduling system schedules it, the modality's worklist query brings it to the console, the study is acquired and sent to the archive, it is read, and the report returns to the record."},
{id:"u10l6-5",t:"u10l6",q:"After a modality upgrade, images and reports flow normally but dose records have stopped arriving. How should you treat that?",a:["As an interface fault with a compliance consequence, belonging on the post-change test list","As cosmetic, since the images are unaffected","As a detector calibration problem","As a display calibration problem"],c:0,e:"Modern systems send a structured dose record the way they send an image, so dose can be totalled, compared with reference levels, and submitted to a registry. A silent stop is the classic post-upgrade failure and it carries regulatory weight."},

// u10l7
{id:"u10l7-1",t:"u10l7",q:"What is the most useful question to ask at the start of any imaging informatics call?",a:["Which vendor supplied the equipment","How wide the fault is: one node or everything at once","What the last preventive maintenance visit found","Whether the department is busy today"],c:1,e:"The width tells you the layer, the likely owner, and the next test before you walk anywhere. One modality failing is that node; every modality failing at once is upstream, and your contribution there is evidence."},
{id:"u10l7-2",t:"u10l7",q:"Every modality in the department lost its worklist at the same moment. Where is the fault?",a:["In each modality's own query configuration","In the detector of each system","Upstream, at the scheduling system, the interface engine, or name resolution","At the reading workstations"],c:2,e:"A fault common to every node is upstream of every node. One modality losing its worklist is that node's query keys, modality code, date range, or title; all of them at once is the shared source or the broker."},
{id:"u10l7-3",t:"u10l7",q:"During a name resolution outage, a few older modalities keep sending images normally. What explains that?",a:["They use a different imaging standard","They are on a separate electrical supply","They cache the archive's name locally forever","Their destinations are configured by address rather than by name"],c:3,e:"Anything configured by address does not need a name resolved, which is both a diagnostic clue and the reason some older entries are deliberately still written as addresses."},
{id:"u10l7-4",t:"u10l7",q:"A secure interface that worked yesterday fails this morning and nobody made any change. What is a very likely cause?",a:["An expired certificate","A failed detector","A full archive disk","A damaged patch cord"],c:0,e:"Certificates expire on a date nobody chose, which produces a failure with no change by anybody at a moment nobody picked. The fix is a calendar entry with a reminder, because the failure is entirely predictable."},
{id:"u10l7-5",t:"u10l7",q:"What is the minimum test after a configuration change, board swap, or upgrade on a networked modality?",a:["A single test exposure on a phantom","A chain test: verify the association, pull a worklist, acquire a test study, confirm it arrives, confirm the progress status moved, and confirm the dose record arrived","A visual inspection of the network cable","A reboot of the console"],c:1,e:"A change to a networked modality is a change to every system it talks to. The classic silent failure is a system that images and sends perfectly and quietly stops producing dose records, which nobody notices until an audit."},

// u10l8
{id:"u10l8-1",t:"u10l8",q:"A workstation is going back to the vendor for credit. Which action is required before it leaves the building?",a:["Removal of the network cable and the keyboard","A final electrical safety test","Documented sanitization of the stored patient data, or a documented removal under an agreement with the vendor","Replacement of the graphics card"],c:2,e:"Patient data lives in local study lists, caches, dose reports, exports, and service logs, not only in the archive. Sanitization before disposal, trade-in, or vendor return is a documented step, and a vendor touching patient data works under a business associate agreement."},
{id:"u10l8-2",t:"u10l8",q:"The security rule asks for three categories of safeguard. What are they?",a:["Hardware, software, and firmware","Preventive, corrective, and predictive","Clinical, financial, and operational","Administrative, physical, and technical"],c:3,e:"Administrative covers policies, training, access review, and risk analysis; physical covers locked rooms, screen placement, and controlled media; technical covers unique accounts, encryption, audit logs, and automatic logoff."},
{id:"u10l8-3",t:"u10l8",q:"A fluoroscopy system runs an operating system the manufacturer will not let you patch for several months. What is the standard compensating control?",a:["Segment it onto its own network segment, reachable only from the addresses and ports it needs","Patch it anyway and document the deviation","Disconnect the system from the network entirely","Install any available antivirus product immediately"],c:0,e:"Segmentation is how the gap between a published vulnerability and a validated patch is covered. Antivirus runs only where the manufacturer supports it with the exclusions it lists, and allow-listing is the usual substitute on a fixed-function device."},
{id:"u10l8-4",t:"u10l8",q:"Why does whole-disk encryption matter so much on a laptop that leaves the hospital with service data on it?",a:["It makes the laptop faster at transferring studies","A lost device that was encrypted is an incident to investigate, while losing one that was never encrypted is a reportable breach","Encryption is required for the device to join the wireless network","It removes the need for a password on the account"],c:1,e:"Encryption in transit protects data on the wire and encryption at rest protects the disk. The practical difference on a loss is between an investigation and a notification duty, which is why portable devices and anything going out for service are encrypted."},
{id:"u10l8-5",t:"u10l8",q:"You believe a modality console has been compromised by malware. What is the correct order of actions?",a:["Power it off at once, then call the vendor","Run a full antivirus scan and return it to use if nothing is found","Isolate it from the network, preserve the evidence, notify security and the manager, and do not power it off if forensics may be needed","Reimage the console immediately to restore service"],c:2,e:"Isolation stops the spread while preserving what investigators need. Powering off can destroy evidence held in memory, so that decision belongs to the security team, and the device stays out of clinical use until it is cleared."},

// u10l9
{id:"u10l9-1",t:"u10l9",q:"An auditor asks the biomed shop for the one document every other security record depends on. Which is it?",a:["The vendor service contract file","The preventive maintenance completion report","The radiation safety committee minutes","The networked medical device inventory with versions"],c:3,e:"A risk assessment cannot be done without an accurate list of what is on the network, what it runs, who services it, and whether it holds patient data. When the inventory is stale, every document downstream of it is wrong."},
{id:"u10l9-2",t:"u10l9",q:"Which information does a manufacturer disclosure statement for medical device security provide?",a:["What data the device stores and transmits, how it authenticates and logs, who may patch it, and how remote service works","The device's radiation output tolerances","The expected service life and resale value","The preventive maintenance interval and parts list"],c:0,e:"Ask for it at purchase and attach it to the device record, then pair it with the software component list the manufacturer can supply, which is what lets you answer whether an advisory naming a software library applies to your device."},
{id:"u10l9-3",t:"u10l9",q:"Which standard governs risk management for information technology networks that carry medical devices?",a:["IEC 60601-1","IEC 80001","NFPA 99","21 CFR 1020"],c:1,e:"Its premise is that neither the network team nor the device team can manage that risk alone, so it asks for a documented joint process with named responsibility. It does not set an acceptable level of risk for you; the organization does that."},
{id:"u10l9-4",t:"u10l9",q:"A vendor engineer wants permanent remote access to a scanner so support calls are faster. What is the controlled arrangement?",a:["A dedicated modem on the scanner, available at all times","A shared vendor account with a strong password","Access through the facility's managed gateway, with a named account, enabled per session, approved and observed, and logged","Remote access through the department's general wireless network"],c:2,e:"Uncontrolled vendor access is one of the easiest findings a surveyor can write. Behind the five controls sits a business associate agreement with any vendor that can see patient data, which compliance will ask about before a credential is issued."},
{id:"u10l9-5",t:"u10l9",q:"Where should the evidence of security patching, configuration changes, and advisory handling live so it can be produced on request?",a:["In the technician's personal notes","In the department manager's email archive","In the vendor's own service portal only","In the CMMS, recorded as the work is done, with the security documents attached to each device record"],c:3,e:"A survey asks for the inventory, evidence that security actions are tracked and closed like any corrective action, access and audit records, and advisory handling. All four come out of the maintenance system if you record them as you go rather than during survey week."},

// u10l10
{id:"u10l10-1",t:"u10l10",q:"What is the correct order for working a network fault on a modality?",a:["Link, the device's own configuration, reachability by address, name, then the application","Application, name, reachability, address, link","Name, link, application, address, reachability","Reachability, application, link, name, address"],c:0,e:"Lower layers are cheap to test and are where the faults usually are. Each step is proved before the next is suspected, which is the discipline that separates a ten-minute call from an afternoon of blame."},
{id:"u10l10-2",t:"u10l10",q:"A reachability test returns \"destination host unreachable\" from the modality itself. What does that mean?",a:["The archive is switched off","Your own side has no route, so check the subnet mask and gateway","The echo traffic is being blocked by a firewall","The name server has the wrong entry"],c:1,e:"That reply comes from the device's own side, not the destination, so it is a routing problem at the sender. A request that simply times out means no reply came back, which can be a host that is off, a wrong subnet, or blocked echo traffic."},
{id:"u10l10-3",t:"u10l10",q:"A newly installed room has a correctly wired cable, a good jack, and a link light, yet it can reach nothing at all. What is the MOST likely cause?",a:["The archive is refusing the association","The subnet mask is correct but the address is duplicated","The jack is patched to a port on the wrong VLAN","The patch cord is too long for the standard"],c:2,e:"A good cable in a port on the wrong logical network behaves exactly like a dead network, and it is the commonest fault in a new room. The question to ask the network team is which VLAN that port is in."},
{id:"u10l10-4",t:"u10l10",q:"A modality answers reachability tests perfectly by address and by name but cannot transfer a single image. What should you suspect?",a:["A damaged patch cord","A failed detector","A wrong subnet mask","A blocked port, a stopped service, or a configuration mismatch"],c:3,e:"Reachable both ways proves the network and name resolution. What remains is the application layer: a firewall permitting the address but not the port, a service that is not running, or a mismatch between the two configurations."},
{id:"u10l10-5",t:"u10l10",q:"You have proved the fault lies past your device. What should you give the network team?",a:["The mask, the gateway, the name servers, and the device's own address, the link state, what pings and what does not, the exact application error, the start time, and what changed","A request to check everything on that floor","A copy of the modality's service manual","The vendor's phone number and case number"],c:0,e:"Measurements get another team to act where suspicion does not. Ask two specific questions in return, the VLAN assigned to that port and the rules between those two addresses, and record the ticket number in the equipment record."},

// u10l11
{id:"u10l11-1",t:"u10l11",q:"A modality reports that its association with the archive is being rejected. Which checks come first, and in what order?",a:["Disk space, licence, permissions, then the cable","AE title on both sides, host address, port, then matching SOP classes","Hanging protocols, routing rules, then prefetch","Detector calibration, then the exposure index"],c:1,e:"Three of those four are typed by hand, and a change made at the archive breaks a modality nobody touched, so all four are re-checked after any change on either end. Run the verification test first, because it exercises all four in one step."},
{id:"u10l11-2",t:"u10l11",q:"Studies from one room show as sent on the console, but the archive has none of them and the local queue is growing with retries. What should you check?",a:["The modality's detector gain calibration","The hanging protocols at the reading stations","The destination entry actually used, then the link and the archive's status","The display calibration on the console"],c:2,e:"Sent to a test node or a stale destination is common, and a growing queue with retries points at the link speed, congestion, or an archive that is down. Read the modality's log and the archive's log together, lined up by timestamp."},
{id:"u10l11-3",t:"u10l11",q:"Only one of the department's six modalities has an empty worklist this morning. Where is the fault?",a:["At the scheduling system, which must be filtering that room","At the interface engine's outbound queue","In name resolution for the whole department","In that node's own worklist query: its keys, modality code, station name, or date range"],c:3,e:"Scope decides the owner. One modality points at its own configuration, where the usual culprits are a modality code the orders do not carry, a date or time zone that excludes today, a changed title at the source, or an unrecognized station name."},
{id:"u10l11-4",t:"u10l11",q:"A study acquired during a worklist outage has landed on the wrong patient. What is the correct action?",a:["Hand it to informatics, who correct it consistently in the archive, the record system, and the report","Delete the study and repeat the examination","Edit the patient details on the modality console and resend","Leave it, since the images are diagnostic either way"],c:0,e:"This is an identifier fault, not a transport fault, and the correction has to be made in several systems at once with an audit trail. A console edit creates a second wrong version rather than fixing the first."},
{id:"u10l11-5",t:"u10l11",q:"Which statement about compression of diagnostic images is correct?",a:["Lossy compression is preferred for archiving because it saves storage","Lossless compression up to about 3:1 is generally accepted, while heavy lossy compression of roughly 10:1 or more supports remote viewing but not computer-aided detection or archiving","Lossless compression cannot reconstruct the original image exactly","Compression ratios above 100:1 are routine for diagnostic reading"],c:1,e:"Lossless compression reconstructs the original exactly and is the acceptable choice for diagnostic images. Heavier lossy compression discards data permanently, which makes it unsuitable for archiving and for automated detection even where it is adequate to look at."},

// u10l12
{id:"u10l12-1",t:"u10l12",q:"A referring clinician asks you to put a study on the monitor in their office so they can make a diagnosis. What is the correct response?",a:["Agree, since the image data is identical on any display","Agree after raising the monitor's brightness to maximum","Explain that the display is not qualified for diagnostic reading and the reading belongs on a calibrated station","Agree if the room lights are turned off"],c:2,e:"A diagnostic display is a calibrated grayscale device in a controlled room with a calibration history; a clinical display is for looking at a study that has already been reported. The distinction is about qualification, not about the image data."},
{id:"u10l12-2",t:"u10l12",q:"What does the grayscale standard display function do?",a:["It compresses images so they transfer faster","It sets the matrix size used by each modality","It corrects each detector pixel's response to a uniform beam","It maps stored pixel values to displayed brightness so equal steps in value look like equal steps in brightness"],c:3,e:"Making a display follow that curve is what calibration means, and it is measured with a luminance meter rather than judged by eye. The point is consistency: the study looks the same on every calibrated station."},
{id:"u10l12-3",t:"u10l12",q:"Measuring a diagnostic reading station, you find its maximum luminance has fallen to 120 cd/m2 and it cannot be calibrated back. What should happen?",a:["Retire it from diagnostic use and move the reading to a qualified station","Leave it in use with a note in the log","Increase the room lighting to compensate","Reduce the image contrast setting in the application"],c:0,e:"Maximum luminance for a diagnostic display should be above 171 cd/m2 and consistent with the manufacturer's figure. A display that cannot be brought back inside tolerance comes out of diagnostic use until it is replaced."},
{id:"u10l12-4",t:"u10l12",q:"Why is the ambient light level part of a display's measurement rather than a matter of preference?",a:["Bright rooms shorten the life of the backlight","Reflected room light adds to every dark pixel and destroys low-contrast visibility","Room light changes the matrix size displayed","Room light affects the network transfer rate"],c:1,e:"A low-contrast test pattern should be equally visible with the lights on as in darkness; if the lights change what can be seen, they are too bright. A reading room sits at about 1 footcandle, which is about 10.8 lux."},
{id:"u10l12-5",t:"u10l12",q:"A reading station's display shows intermittent horizontal lines and occasional flicker. What should you do FIRST?",a:["Replace the display panel","Recalibrate the display to the standard curve","Swap the video cable and try another output on the graphics card","Rebuild the user profile on the workstation"],c:2,e:"Lines, flicker, color casts, and dropouts are the cable, the connector, or the graphics path before they are the panel. Dim overall is the backlight or an aged panel, and uneven brightness is uniformity, which calibration corrects only up to a point."}
);
